WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.610
WESLEY: Hi, and welcome to
"Serverless Migration Station,"

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:04.950
a serverless expedition
miniseries dedicated

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.860
to helping developers modernize
their apps to using the latest

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:11.190
features available to one of our
serverless compute platforms.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:12.990
Today, we're
continuing our coverage

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.570
of migrating from App Engine
Task Queues to Cloud Tasks.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:19.020
MARTIN: Sounds great, and
I'm so happy to be here, Wes.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:22.530
Last time, in module 7, we
added App Engine Task Queues,

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:27.840
specifically push tasks, to
our sample App Engine NDB Flask

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:28.690
app.

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:29.835
So what are we doing today?

00:00:29.835 --> 00:00:31.260
WESLEY: Oh, that's
right, Martin.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.630
For those who haven't
seen the module 7 video

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.510
or completed its codelab,
please pause here to do so,

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.270
because we pick up from
where module 7 leaves off.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:41.670
Today we're migrating
from App Engine

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:46.080
NDB to Cloud NDB and App Engine
Task Queues to Cloud Tasks.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.550
All the links you
need are down below.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:49.800
MARTIN: Now, wait, Wes.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.840
Did you say we're migrating from
App Engine NDB and App Engine

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:57.210
Task Queues to Cloud
NDB and Cloud Tasks?

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.542
Isn't that two migrations?

00:00:59.542 --> 00:01:00.750
WESLEY: That's right, Martin.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.330
While the code will
undergo both migrations,

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.720
we're only focused on App Engine
Task Queues to Cloud Tasks,

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.450
because moving from App
Engine NDB to Cloud NDB

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.670
was already covered in module 2.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:14.700
Modernizing your apps means
moving off of the legacy App

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.280
Engine services like
NDB and Task Queues

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.730
to standalone services like
Cloud NDB and Cloud Tasks.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:22.740
So we're doing both
at the same time,

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:26.520
preparing our app to upgrade
to Python 3 next in module 9.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.970
So for all of you, the same
drill as earlier with module 7.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:33.570
If you haven't seen the module 2
video or completed its codelab,

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.378
we invite you to
pause and do that here

00:01:35.378 --> 00:01:37.920
so you're familiar with it as
we're going to concentrate only

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.210
on Task Queues here.

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:39.870
MARTIN: Wow.

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:43.192
That's a lot of review, but
I think I'm caught up now.

00:01:43.192 --> 00:01:44.400
WESLEY: That's great, Martin.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:45.870
Now that you're
up to speed, let's

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.700
talk about what we're doing.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.650
Remember the code
from module 7 where

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.860
we deleted all visits older
than the oldest displayed

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:53.880
from Datastore?

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.950
Well, we're going to
keep that functionality,

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.350
but moving the code
to Cloud Tasks.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.350
Since it's a standalone product,
there's a little bit more setup

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:02.190
to do.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:04.980
In addition, we're also
migrating from Engine NDB

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.590
to Cloud NDB, as
mentioned before.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:08.800
MARTIN: Sounds good.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.710
I'm going to do this by hand
while you are explaining it.

00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:14.970
So would you please point me
to the code and the codelab?

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.200
WESLEY: Oh, for sure, Martin.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.900
If you did the module
7 codelab, please start

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.110
with your own code base.

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:23.190
But if you didn't, clone the
repo or download the zip file.

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:25.110
We're going to start
with the module 7 code

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.840
and finish with what's
in the module 8 folder.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.180
Here's the codelab as well,
so pause one more time

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:31.630
to get everything you need.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:33.630
Once you're ready,
let's go to the computer

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:34.500
and migrate it now.

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:40.590
If you don't have a confirmed
working module 7 app yet,

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:43.560
upload to App Engine
with gcloud app deploy.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.780
Most users watching this
video will be on Python 2.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:50.290
So if that's the case, ensure
your lib folder is ready to go.

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:52.890
If not, or you're unsure,
delete what you have

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:56.010
and run pip install
so that you have it.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.890
Confirm the app's the
same as from module 7,

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.990
whether it's your own or ours.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.910
Once you have, then
you're ready to go.

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:07.300
Changes to the config files are
nearly identical to the changes

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:08.770
made in module 2.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.590
We're adding use of the
Google Cloud client libraries,

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.890
and these changes include adding
required built-in packages that

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:19.220
go into app.yaml instead
of requirements.txt.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.530
Now, any time built-in
libraries are used,

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:23.060
a few more lines
of code have to be

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:26.130
added to appengine_config
to point your app to them,

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:29.430
so make these changes as well.

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:33.020
Now let's do the non-built-in
libraries, like Cloud NDB.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:37.460
In module 2, we added Cloud
NDB to requirements.txt.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:41.240
This time we're also going to
add the Cloud Tasks library.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:44.030
Python 2 users should now
delete their lib folder

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.340
and run the pip install
command to install

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:50.720
Flask, Cloud NDB, and Cloud
Tasks to the lib folder.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.360
Again, these updates are
just like in module 2,

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:56.630
but adding both Cloud
NDB and Cloud Tasks.

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:59.450
Now let's move to main.py.

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:03.290
At the top, swap out the imports
of App Engine's Task Queue

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:06.530
and NDB libraries and replacing
them with the Google Cloud

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:07.820
equivalents.

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:11.215
The payload sent to Cloud
Tasks needs to be JSON encoded,

00:04:11.215 --> 00:04:13.340
so we're going to have to
use that standard library

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:15.210
module as well.

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:18.050
Next, most cloud
libraries require a client

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:19.670
for each service you're using--

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:22.940
in our case, Cloud
NDB and Cloud Tasks.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:26.420
More specifically, Cloud
Tasks requires a project ID

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:29.030
and an apps region along
with the queue name.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:31.610
Now, this wasn't required
with App Engine Task Queues,

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.280
but recognize that
Cloud Tasks is now

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:36.680
a separate product that can
work with both App Engine

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:40.310
and not App Engine apps,
hence the extra red tape.

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:43.490
Fortunately, its client
provides a convenience function

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:46.880
named QUEUE_PATH that builds
a fully qualified path

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:48.300
string for us.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.270
Be sure to swap in
your app's values

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:53.070
into the placeholder constants.

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:56.310
Now, creating a new visit and
querying the most recent visits

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:57.660
doesn't really change.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:59.880
The only difference
is due to Cloud NDB

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.580
requiring use of its
Python context manager

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:04.270
when making Datastore calls.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:07.470
So you'll see a new width
block containing such calls

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:10.530
in both store_visit
and fetch_visits.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:12.580
Review the module
2 codelab and video

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:14.520
if you need more
information here.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:17.820
For us in module 7, things get
really interesting from here

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:19.680
on out, meaning
that once we grab

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.260
the timestamp of the
oldest visit displayed,

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:24.090
we can move on.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.980
With the App Engine
Task Queue library,

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.170
it's a single call
to create a push task

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.150
and respond back to the user.

00:05:30.150 --> 00:05:32.910
With Cloud Tasks, it's
also a single call.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:35.340
However, there's a
little bit more setup.

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:37.480
After logging the
oldest time stamp,

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:40.170
you see the necessary
payload that Cloud Tasks

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:44.490
needs, a flag indicating an
App Engine request, a handler

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:48.420
URI, JSON-encoded parameters,
and an HTTP header.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.720
Pass this data, plus the Task
Queue path from the above,

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:53.280
to create task.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.400
That's it, and the
return doesn't change.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:00.940
As far as the task goes,
not much change here either.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:04.240
Yes, pulling out the parameter
is slightly different.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:06.940
Any Datastore code is
within a with blog.

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:10.000
But querying, logging, and
deletion of old entities

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.740
is pretty much the same.

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:14.500
There are very few changes
here because it's mostly still

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:18.100
the same HTTP POST method
we had in module 7.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:21.760
Cloud Tasks is just an
alternative execution platform.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:24.940
You really shouldn't need to
change anything in the handler.

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:28.120
Finally, there are no changes
to the main application handler

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:29.230
route.

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:31.120
Ensure you've made all
the updates we just

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:32.530
covered to your sample app.

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:34.810
After uploading with
gcloud app deploy,

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:37.040
it should run exactly
the same as before,

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:39.400
except now it uses Cloud Tasks.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.340
If you're using no other
legacy App Engine services,

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:45.100
it is now safe to port
your app to Python 3.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:48.210
Congratulations.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:48.780
MARTIN: Cool.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:50.640
Thanks for showing us that, Wes.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:54.330
I'm surprised how similar Cloud
Tasks is to App Engine push

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:55.620
tasks.

